-- title:
--   Square of the ramified prime of a maximal order is rΛ
-- statement:
--   Fix rationals $a,b$ and natural numbers $q,q'$, each assumed prime, and consider the quaternion algebra $B=\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$. Assume `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`, i.e. $0<a$ or $0<b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ the completion $B\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra (every nonzero element is a unit) precisely when $q\in v$ or $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda\subseteq B$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule which is a maximal order: it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $B$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, is finitely generated over $\mathbb{Z}$, and any order containing it equals it. Let $r\in\mathbb{N}$ with $r=q$ or $r=q'$, and write $\mathfrak{P}_r=\{x\in\Lambda:\ \mathrm{nrd}(x)\in r\mathbb{Z}\}$, where $\mathrm{nrd}(x)=x_{\mathrm{re}}^2-a\,x_{i}^2-b\,x_{j}^2+ab\,x_{k}^2$. The conclusion is a conjunction: first, for all $x,x'\in\mathfrak{P}_r$ there is $y\in\Lambda$ with $x x'=r\cdot y$; second, for every $y\in\Lambda$ the element $r\cdot y$ lies in the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of the set of products $x x'$ with $x,x'\in\mathfrak{P}_r$. Thus $\mathfrak{P}_r^2=r\Lambda$ as $\mathbb{Z}$-modules.
--
--   This is the classical fact that in a maximal order of a quaternion algebra the two-sided ideal above a ramified prime $r$ has square $r\Lambda$, the local statement being that the radical of $\Lambda\otimes\mathbb{Z}_r$ is generated by a uniformiser of norm $r$. It is used in the construction of the Atkin–Lehner involution at a ramified prime, where $w_r^2$ acts through multiplication by $r$, and is cited in the identification of Atkin–Lehner quotients of fake elliptic curves.
